Focusing on "property rights" here confuses the issue.  Nethack is an
open source game, in practice enthusiastically developed for by an
active fan base.  I would venture that significantly more time and
work has gone into unofficial nethack coding that that produced by the
moribund devteam - and certainly more results are published per unit
time, much of it high quality.  By several measurements, the devteam
does a poor job of maintaining the game, specifically considering the
high interest level in the game and in seeing repairs and new features
for it.  Whose decisions and opinions deserve more weight here?

If abstract "ownership" of the game name, from the early 1990s, by a
Dutch national technical research institute and one person apparently
associated or formerly associated wth it (whether on this devteam or
not), means more to you than what people actually give the game
vibrancy and life now, then I think your priorities are misplaced
here.

